Drop ExprParseFile's counted reference to its source accessor.

The second half of huggorm#128, beside nix-expr-path-holds-no-accessor.
EvalState::evalFile allocates one ExprParseFile per `import` with `new`,
and the class derives from `gc`, so Boehm frees it without running its
destructor. Its `SourcePath path` held `ref<SourceAccessor>`, usually
rootFS, so the reference was never dropped. rootFS mounts the store
accessor, which holds the Store: once an evaluator imported a file, its
Store and daemon connections outlived it for the life of the process.
Measured on the ExprPath-patched libnixexpr: one leaked daemon socket per
closed evaluator that ran `(import (toFile "a.nix" "{ x = 1; }")).x`;
gdb shows ~EvalState and never ~LocalStore. `scopedImport`, which does
not go through evalFile, does not leak.

The accessor is now a raw pointer and the counted reference is rebuilt
from `shared_from_this()` when the thunk is forced, which happens only
while the EvalState that owns the accessor lives. An accessor nothing
else owned would throw bad_weak_ptr there, not dangle.

--- a/src/libexpr/eval.cc	2026-10-06 15:54:15.274027300 +0200
+++ b/src/libexpr/eval.cc	2026-10-06 15:54:22.893097854 +0200
@@ -1080,17 +1080,23 @@
 struct ExprParseFile : Expr, gc
 {
     // FIXME: make this a reference (see below).
-    SourcePath path;
+    // Not a SourcePath: a `gc` object runs no destructor, so a counted
+    // accessor here would never be released. The EvalState owns it.
+    SourceAccessor * accessor;
+    CanonPath canonPath;
     bool mustBeTrivial;
 
     ExprParseFile(SourcePath & path, bool mustBeTrivial)
-        : path(path)
+        : accessor(&*path.accessor)
+        , canonPath(path.path)
         , mustBeTrivial(mustBeTrivial)
     {
     }
 
     void eval(EvalState & state, Env & env, Value & v) override
     {
+        SourcePath path{ref<SourceAccessor>(accessor->shared_from_this()), canonPath};
+
         printTalkative("evaluating file '%s'", path);
 
         auto e = state.parseExprFromFile(path);
